# Derivatives: Measuring Instantaneous Change

> The derivative of $f(x)$ at a point is the slope of the curve there —
> how fast the output changes per unit input at that exact moment.

## 1. From Secant to Tangent

The average rate over an interval $h$ is
$\frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}$. Shrinking $h \to 0$ turns the secant's slope
into the tangent's:

@math label="eq:derivative-def"
f'(x) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}
@/math

## 2. Rules That Replace the Definition

| Function | Derivative | Rule name |
| --- | --- | --- |
| $c$ (constant) | 0 | constant rule |
| $x^n$ | $nx^{n-1}$ | power rule |
| $\sin x$ | $\cos x$ | standard |
| $\cos x$ | $-\sin x$ | watch the sign |
| $e^x$ | $e^x$ | unchanged by differentiation |
| $\ln x$ | $1/x$ | standard |

## Example

For $f(x) = 3x^4 - 2x + 7$:

@math label="eq:power-example"
f'(x) = 12x^3 - 2
@/math

The derivative of the constant 7 vanishes — constants don't change.

## 3. What the Sign Tells You

@diagram flowchart id="sign-reading" layout="hierarchical" aria="Reading the sign of the derivative"
node pos label="f'(x) > 0" shape="round-rect" fill="#22c55e"
node neg label="f'(x) < 0" shape="round-rect"
node zero label="f'(x) = 0" shape="diamond"

pos -> zero label="rising, then flattens"
neg -> zero label="falling, then flattens"

edge zero -> pos style="dashed" label="minimum possible turn"
edge zero -> neg style="dashed" label="maximum possible turn"
@/diagram

- Positive derivative → increasing; negative → decreasing.
- Zero derivative marks a **stationary point** (peak, valley, or flat).
  The **second derivative** sorts them: $f'' > 0$ minimum, $f'' < 0$
  maximum.

## Example: optimisation in one move

Height of a thrown ball: $h(t) = 20t - 5t^2$. Then
$h'(t) = 20 - 10t$, and $h' = 0$ at $t = 2$ s — the peak. Height there:
$h(2) = 40 - 20 = 20$ m.

## 4. Rates in the Real World

If $s(t)$ is distance, $s'(t)$ is velocity. If $C(x)$ is cost,
$C'(x)$ is marginal cost — the extra cost of one more unit. Same
machinery, different nouns.

[important title="Differentiable implies continuous"]
A function must be unbroken at a point to have a derivative there.
The converse fails: $|x|$ is continuous everywhere but has no
derivative at $0$ (the corner has no single tangent direction).
[/important]

## Key Takeaways

- Derivative = limit of average rates = slope of the tangent line.
- Power rule covers polynomials; sign of $f'$ gives rise/fall.
- Stationary points need the second derivative to classify.
